                                            "take":  "Baron\u0027s story is compelling, but it\u0027s worth naming the selection bias embedded in it: his record is inseparable from two of the most extraordinary wealth-creation events in modern market history (Tesla and SpaceX), both tied to a single founder. The broader lesson Brown seems to want to draw Ã¢â‚¬â€ that patient, high-conviction, founder-focused investing works Ã¢â‚¬â€ is true, but survivorship bias makes it hard to recommend as a replicable strategy. The more actionable insight for institutional allocators is not \u0027be like Baron\u0027 but rather: what structural features of Baron Capital (long lock-ups, aligned incentives, tolerance for tracking error) allowed him to hold when others couldn\u0027t?",
