                                            "title":  "Santoli: Why investors should be on high alert heading into September",
                                            "source":  "Mike Santoli (CNBC)",
                                            "author":  "Mike Santoli",
                                            "url":  "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/31/santoli-why-investors-should-be-on-high-alert-heading-into-september.html",
                                            "date":  "2026-08-31",
                                            "imageUrl":  null,
                                            "summary":  "Santoli argues that the case for caution entering September is not purely seasonal but structural: key market metrics Ã¢â‚¬â€ VIX, yields, commodity indexes, and credit spreads Ã¢â‚¬â€ are simultaneously coiling near consequential thresholds, and a breach of any one could signal a change in market character. He notes the VIX has slipped below 15, broad commodity indexes are near five-year highs, and corporate credit spreads are remarkably tight, leaving little margin for error. Evenly split Fed rate-hike odds weeks before a policy meeting add a further headwind by discouraging incremental risk-taking.",
                                            "take":  "Santoli is doing something underrated here: disaggregating the seasonal narrative from the structural one. September\u0027s bad reputation is well-known and already discounted by sophisticated investors Ã¢â‚¬â€ what matters is the underlying market plumbing, and he\u0027s right that the simultaneous compression of VIX, credit spreads, and commodity risk into a narrow range creates fragility. When multiple risk premia are suppressed at once, the correlations that normally provide portfolio diversification tend to spike precisely when you need them least. The actionable read: this is a poor risk/reward environment for adding concentrated equity exposure, regardless of what the seasonal calendar says.",
