                                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                                            "imageUrl":  null,
                                            "summary":  "Santoli examines why bond markets are pushing borrowing costs to multi-year highs despite massive institutional demand for debt issuance from governments and corporations. He traces the tension between the AI buildout sector\u0027s insatiable need for financing Ã¢â‚¬â€ targeting roughly $2 trillion in compute capacity by end of next year Ã¢â‚¬â€ and the bond market\u0027s role as a pressure valve meant to moderate borrowing across the broader economy. Treasury yields moved sharply on reports that the Treasury Department could deploy its $1 trillion General Account for bond buybacks, briefly pulling the 10-year yield down more than 7 basis points to 4.625%.",
                                            "take":  "Santoli is surfacing a genuine macroeconomic tension that most equity-focused investors are underweighting: the AI capex supercycle is effectively competing with the housing market, small business credit, and consumer borrowing for a finite supply of capital, and that competition is showing up in the yield curve. The Treasury\u0027s General Account maneuver as a yield management tool is a significant policy signal Ã¢â‚¬â€ it suggests the government is more sensitive to long-end rates than official communications let on. For enterprise AI buyers, the implication is that the cost of capital for competitors and suppliers is rising even as AI investment accelerates, which could accelerate vendor consolidation faster than the technology adoption curve alone would predict.",
                                            "isNew":  true
