                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran argues that venture capital\u0027s obsession with scaling revenues over building profitable business models has become a structural flaw in the asset class. While rapid scaling makes sense for businesses with strong unit economics and favorable market dynamics, many startups chase growth at all costs because investor incentives reward exit valuations rather than sustainable profitability. The glut of capital from both VC and public equity markets has enabled companies to reach massive public-market valuations while remaining deeply unprofitable and lacking durable business models.",
                                                              "take":  "Damodaran is essentially calling out a multi-decade incentive misalignment: VCs profit on exits, not on whether the underlying business ever earns a return on capital. For public-market investors, this is a critical filter ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â a high-revenue, fast-growing company spinning out of VC backing deserves heavy scrutiny of its unit economics before any premium valuation is awarded. The real money in the next cycle may go to investors disciplined enough to distinguish genuine compounders from well-funded scale stories.",
